\section{Several terms and distribution parameters}{

\code{sc_additive} adapts mgcv's example. Its raw terms are \code{gw_f0(x0)},
\code{2 * plogis(20 * (x1 - 0.4))}, \code{gw_f2(x2)} and \code{10 * (x3 - 0.55)^4}.
The true predictor is their sum minus 1, divided by 4. The second term is
increasing and the fourth is convex. Use \code{predictors = function(d) d$f}
with \code{family = poisson()} to sample mgcv's Poisson example.

\code{sc_tensor} uses \code{f = x + z + x * z}, increasing in both covariates. Its
\code{dx} and \code{dz} columns give the true partial derivatives.

\code{sc_location_scale} uses log mean \code{sin(pi * x) / 2} and log dispersion
\code{-2 + z}, returned in \code{f} and \code{f_scale}. It defaults to \code{gammals()} and
records derivatives in \code{d1}, \code{d2} and \code{d_scale}. Constraints act on the
specified predictors; in particular the concavity concerns the log mean.

The recipes use the default \code{bs = 0} for fitting without bootstrap
replicates. To obtain native constraint-respecting bootstrap coefficient
draws, fit with a positive \code{bs}, such as \code{bs = 100}.
}

